        <title type="html"><![CDATA[Fix default close code for WebSocket.close() with no arguments]]></title>
        <id>https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/46475</id>
        <link href="https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/46475"/>
        <updated>2026-11-11T00:57:10.000Z</updated>
        <content type="html"><![CDATA[<img src="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/141590436?v=4" width="64" height="64" alt=""/><br/><h3 dir="auto">Description</h3>
<p dir="auto">Corrects the <code class="notranslate">code</code> parameter docs for <code class="notranslate">WebSocket.close()</code>. When <code class="notranslate">close()</code> is called without a <code class="notranslate">code</code>, the close frame is sent with no status code, so the server sees <code class="notranslate">1005</code> ("No Status Received"), not <code class="notranslate">1000</code>. The page now also explains that the <code class="notranslate">code</code> of the browser's <code class="notranslate">close</code> event is taken from the server's reply.</p>
<h3 dir="auto">Motivation</h3>
<p dir="auto">The page currently says an omitted <code class="notranslate">code</code> is set to <code class="notranslate">1000</code> for a normal closure. No code is sent at all. Readers who rely on the current text (for example, checking for <code class="notranslate">1000</code> on the server) get unexpected results. The browser-side <code class="notranslate">code</code> also depends on how the server replies, which the page didn't mention.</p>
<p dir="auto">I verified this in Chrome and Firefox, and with Node's built-in <code class="notranslate">WebSocket</code>, against a raw server that logs the exact close frame it receives:</p>
<ul dir="auto">
<li>In all cases, the client's close frame had an empty payload (no status code).</li>
<li>The <code class="notranslate">close</code> event <code class="notranslate">code</code> mirrors the server's reply: <code class="notranslate">1005</code> when the server echoes the empty frame, <code class="notranslate">1000</code> when the server replies with <code class="notranslate">1000</code>, and <code class="notranslate">1006</code> (<code class="notranslate">wasClean: false</code>) when the server drops the connection without replying.</li>
</ul>
<h3 dir="auto">Additional details</h3>
<ul dir="auto">
<li><a href="https://websockets.spec.whatwg.org/#dom-websocket-close" rel="nofollow">WHATWG WebSockets spec, <code class="notranslate">close()</code></a>: if neither <code class="notranslate">code</code> nor <code class="notranslate">reason</code> is present, "the WebSocket Close message must not have a body".</li>
<li><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455#section-7.1.5" rel="nofollow">RFC 6455 §7.1.5</a>: the connection close code is the status code in the first close frame <em>received</em>. If that frame contains no status code, the close code is considered to be <code class="notranslate">1005</code>.</li>
<li><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455#section-5.5.1" rel="nofollow">RFC 6455 §5.5.1</a>: an endpoint replying to a close frame typically echoes the status code it received.</li>
</ul>
<h3 dir="auto">Related issues and pull requests</h3>
<p dir="auto">None.</p>]]></content>
